import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
---
<BaseLayout title="CMB as Temporal Origin" description="Cross-sectional model showing time beginning at the CMB.">
  <article class="container" style="padding-top: 4rem; padding-bottom: 4rem; max-width: 800px; margin: 0 auto;">
    <h1>The CMB as Temporal Origin</h1>
    <p class="lead">Standard cosmology places T=0 at the singularity. In this mechanical view, measurable temporal sequence begins at the Cosmic Microwave Background (CMB).</p>

    <figure style="margin: 2rem 0; text-align: center;">
      <!-- Replace filename with your actual image -->
      <img src="/images/cmb-boundary-model.jpg" alt="Cross-section model showing CMB as T=0" style="width: 100%; border-radius: 12px; box-shadow: 0 4px 20px rgba(0,0,0,0.1);" />
      <figcaption style="color: #64748b; font-size: 0.9rem; margin-top: 0.5rem;">Fig 1. Observable timeline begins at the CMB. The region beyond represents the pre-temporal void.</figcaption>
    </figure>

    <h2>Core Mechanics</h2>
    <ul style="line-height: 1.8; color: #334155;">
      <li><strong>Pre-CMB Void:</strong> A state without measurable temporal sequence. Expansion occurs, but without a reference frame for "before" and "after."</li>
      <li><strong>Recombination (T=0):</strong> Neutral atoms form, allowing photons to travel freely. This is the first moment light can carry temporal information, marking the onset of measurable time.</li>
      <li><strong>Post-CMB Flow:</strong> Matter moves through the established temporal substrate. Mass creates resistance to this flow, producing gravitational time dilation.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Observational Implication</h2>
    <p>Looking toward the CMB is not looking "back in time"—it is observing the <em>edge of temporal structure</em>. The isotropy of the CMB reflects the uniform onset of time across the observable universe, not a central explosion point.</p>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>